A||||28||88 Fiero GT Turbo||Intercoolers||||5||
Z||000000||88 Fiero GT Turbo||09-11-2000||05:07 PM||||I was wondering how many of you guys with turbo's in your Fiero have an intercooler setup with it.  As you know my 88 GT Turbo is being upgraded to a 3.4 liter and keeping my turbo and wanted to know if anyone can help me out in intergrading an intercooler into the setup.  My goal is to run 15 psi of boost but I know that it must be intercooled.  If anyone can help with some pictures or websites that would be great.  Thanks for all your help.<P>Later,<BR>Jack<P>88 Fiero GT Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfixit58||09-11-2000||05:20 PM||||Jack,<P>Although I don't have a turbo (yet), I have done a little research in this area. I talked to a local company called Majestic Turbo about the Fiero engine configuration (motor in back, limited space, etc) and they recommended a water-to-air cooler. It's a closed loop system with it's own coolant, circulating pump and forced fan. You could build one small ehough to fit where the battery is now and, of course, move the battery up front. Majestic advertises in "Turbo" magazine. Just check the news stands. They are nice guys who don't seem to mind helping.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||88 Fiero GT Turbo||09-11-2000||05:26 PM||||Roy thanks for the reply but for the amout of boost that I am tring for I think a air to air intercooler would be better for the job.||||reg||14
Z||000003||CW85T-Type||09-11-2000||05:55 PM||||FWIW, Water to Air intercoolers are more efficent than Air to Air.  This efficency comes at a cost though.  Water to Air setups cost more.<P>Who says you can't run 15psi without intercooling?  My 1985 Regal T-Type ran 16psi on 92 octane pump gas (albeit with an 8:1 compression ratio and very low timing).<P>Intercooling will give you a greatly reduced inlet charge no matter what though.  <P>Chris Wilson<BR>1985 Fiero SE V6<BR>1985 T-Type||||reg||1
Z||000004||N_Man||09-11-2000||06:07 PM||||15 PSI really isnt that much boost. many cars come stock running 15PSI with no intercooler (eg. Merkur XR4TI 5spd, autos ran 10) Intercoolers definitly help cool the charge & make more power, but if i had to guess you could run 15PSI with no intercooler, assuming the engine is built for a turbo.<P>Now im not saying dont use one, I would personally consider it the best upgrade you could do to a turbo.<P>But what i am saying is that for 15PSI water to air should work respectably. Plus considering the fieros design it is the best bet (remember the more pipe you run air through the more the pressure drops and turbo lag you create)<P>If I were to try i would remove the air box replace with a cone mounted low, put the water to air cooler in place of the air box.  Move the battery to the front and mount the water resivour and a pump there.  I would also want to put a deck lid scoupe in and jet coat all exaust parts to further help cool the engine compartment.<P>Hope that helps||||reg||1
Z||000005||88 Fiero GT Turbo||09-11-2000||06:32 PM||||Sorry guys I guess I was miss informed about the intercooling.  I thought that the water in the water to air the water would get hot and the air to air you always have fresh cool air to the intercooler<P>Later,<BR>Jack<P>88 GT Turbo Turbo||||reg||11
Z||000006||hugh||09-11-2000||07:55 PM||||If you were running boost for a prolonged time with any intercooler the temperature of your inlet air will rise due to the volume of heat generated and the limited ability of any intercooler to remove all the heat.A large cooler will remove more heat from the air charge than a small one due mainly to the mass of the cooler itself.A water to air cooler will remove more heat than an air to air,since it is about 9 times more efficient at removing heat. The water system also needs it's own radiator. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||09-11-2000||07:56 PM||||I had an idea not too long ago about just the thing you want to do.<BR>      What fluid, already in your car, has all the necessary apparatus associated with it to move heat around? I'm not talking about engine coolant.<BR>      FREON.<BR>      I haven't done the thermodynamic calculations yet to see how much heat would have to be moved, but in theory, you could increase your air conditioner's condenser capacity by 2-3x and custom fab a second evaporator (in parallel with the normal evaporator so that you keep A/C) to act as an intercooler. This intercooler could be column shaped, and go where the battery is now. The battery would go up front under the spare tire, and the battery tray would be cut out to make more room for the intercooler.<BR>     The intercooler itself would be a box with a bunch of air tubes snaking around a bunch of freon tubes. Water could fill up the space between the tubes and increase the "thermal mass" of the system, slowing down rapid changes in temperature and providing more consistent performance.<BR>     You could use refrigeration electronics to control the system based on inlet air temp. In theory, with enough capacity in the system, engine inlet air could be BELOW ambient air temp, and stay at or below a CONSTANT level, year round, for very consistent performance regardless of weather.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||zeusrekning||09-11-2000||08:12 PM||||Can someone tell me what exactly an intercooler does. looks like . and how it works . i think i have an idea but vague.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Loki||09-11-2000||09:00 PM||||An intercooler is like a radiator for the turbo..But instead of hot water its hot air.....When I go turbo im not going turbo, but water injection...It will be set to come on at a set psi say 15-20 and it uses a high pressure pump that shoots a mist of water into the air stream close to the TB...And no it doesnt hurt anything..Just a small tank of water the pump, nozzel and a pressure sensor to read the psi........<P>Loki||||reg||1
Z||000010||hugh||09-11-2000||09:18 PM||||Will,It sounds good,but you don't need to get the coolant for the air down that low for street driving.Water cooled to ambient is about as good as it gets,unless you use dry ice and an antifreeze solution for short bursts on the track.An air conditioner compressor couldn't keep up with any prolonged boost generating heat,Also with tubes snaking through the solution I think you would have too much of a pressure drop.<BR>zuesrekning,When you compress anything,but in this case air,you raise it's heat content(temperature).The purpose of the intercooler is to lower the temperature to as close to ambient as possible.In some cases such as on the drag strip with a water to air intercooler the heat transfer medium can be considerably below ambient.The reason to lower the air charge temperature is twofold.#1the lowered temperature reduces the possibility of detonation and #2 the cooler the air is the more dense it is and consequently contains more oxygen,this allows you to put more fuel into the engine and therefore make more power.||||reg||1
Z||000011||hugh||09-11-2000||09:24 PM||||Loki, That will work to lower the temperature of the air charge,but the mist will displace some of the air and therefore some of the oxygen available to burn fuel.You might want to use an alcohol mixture.||||reg||1
Z||000012||zeusrekning||09-11-2000||09:39 PM||||get it so far. Go more in depth.from your description you inject cold air water into the turbo?<BR>i was picturing the pipe out of the turbo being cooled some how???||||reg||1
Z||000013||N_Man||09-11-2000||11:09 PM||||What loki is talking about is water injection and is a totally different concept than intercooling, but achieves a similar result.<P>the turbo sucks in cool air from outside but as it passes through the turbo it heats up.  the intercooler attempts to cool the heated air down.  In the turbo system it fits between the turbo and the engine.<P>Air to air:  Uses air to cool the charge works just like a radiator<P>Liquid to air: uses some kind of liquid to cool the charge down.  The intercooler would be a conductive metal with water flowing around it to keep it cool.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Loki||09-11-2000||11:09 PM||||Gonna used distileld (sp?) water and thats it......Its not sprayed heavy just a mist....race cars use it and others with no loss.......http://www.spearcointercoolers.com/h20inject.htm  thats the link to spearco's system......<P><BR>Loki||||reg||1
Z||000015||lou_dias||09-11-2000||11:31 PM||||Hey, I'm from Fall River, MA.  Not too far from Riverside, RI.  I'd like to see your setup.  What turbo do you have in there?  I have a 3.4 in my Gt.  I'd like to compare a $4000 3.4 conversion to a turbo set up.  I also want to turbo my car eventually as well.<BR>Can we meet sometime?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Will||09-12-2000||12:36 AM||||hugh:<BR>     It was a brainstorm, not a finished product. If it turns out that the system would need a bigger compressor, then I'm sure that someone capable and resourceful enough to attempt such a project could arrange that. If it requires stacking 3 or even 4 Fiero condensers and plumbing them in parallel, then that can also be done. I'm sure someone with the expertise to build this system can design an intercooler box that does not cost much boost.<BR>     Are you street or track? You say that it doesn't need to be that cold for the street, but it couldn't keep up with prolonged boost (track). Which is it?<BR>     It would be great for drag racing. The driver could have a knob in the cockpit that turns down IAT for a little more power, or turn it back up when not running hard to save some parasitic losses. There may be some hurdles to overcome for prolonged boost, but every new idea has hurdles to overcome.<BR>     The compressor is capable of running continuously. As I understand it, the limitations of A/C capacity are more in the condenser/evaporator area.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Phantom Rage||09-12-2000||03:37 AM||||Please keep in mind that most factory turbo charged cars have less compression than that of their natural aspirated counterpart.  In some cases boost is not everything, for instance it might be better to run at lower boost while maintaining your current compression ratio for a better torque curve, thus better streetability..  <P>At 15psi I would worry more about proper fueling and timing than just throwing on an intercooler.  <P>How big is your current turbo?  Higher boost with too small of a turbo = quick spool up, but at a cost of less life, backpressure and higher heat.<P>Too large an intercooler = space, less responsive.<BR>Too small an intercooler = inefficient<BR>Too much plumbing = space, less responsive<P>Check out this guy’s site about how he made his own water to air inter-cooler: <A HREF="http://www.merkurxr4ti.com/awic.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.merkurxr4ti.com/awic.html</A> <P>You may also want to get your turbo upgraded for better efficiency.  <P>P.S. mrfixit58, give C.M.R Turbos a call.  The owner Charlie used to work for Majestic but owns his own shop now.  His prices are way lower and he is a really nice guy too.  (813) 978-8872<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000018||mrfixit58||09-12-2000||07:50 AM||||Hey Phantom,<P>Good to see someone else from Tampa. I'll have to CMR when the time comes. A little off topic but, are you a member of any local Fiero clubs?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||hugh||09-12-2000||07:56 AM||||Will,99% of the time you are driving on the street you are not running positive pressure(boost).You will only get boost when you step on the gas and generate enough exhaust volumn and heat to drive the turbo compressor faster.Using the refrigeration compressor to remove the heat of compression wouldn't work because the evaporator couldn't remove enough heat fast enough.I have a very big intercooler on my GN,have almost no pressure drop,no detonation and no lag.The size of the cooler(mass)is one of the reasons it works so well on short bursts on the strip.<p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 09-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||88 Fiero GT Turbo||09-12-2000||06:54 PM||||Lou Dais,<BR>    I am having my motor done right now by Cotton's Auto Repair out in Agwam, Ma.  All this guy does is Buick Grand National's.  The reason I heard of him is my friend Matt has a Grand National and he had him do some work on it and we got to talking about my car and he wanted to do the motor and make it a project of his.  As for meeting me we can meet anytime that is good for you.  I would love to see your car.  I am usually down in Fall River on Sat. nights doing some racing in the Grand National.  Let me know and I will give you my cell number and you can call sometime.<P>Later,<BR>Jack<P>88 Fiero GT Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000021||Will||09-12-2000||06:58 PM||||Hugh:<BR>     I'm not talking about using a stock Fiero evaporator. I am talking about a custom evaporator possibly built using an existing intercooler, as in the link Phantom Rage posted. A custom unit can be designed to meet certain criteria, one of which could be it's capacity for heat transfer per degree of temeprature difference, another might be minimal throughput restriction.<BR>     The system could be set to maintain IAT at 50 degrees F. It would not have to work very hard to do this for the minimal air volume required by sedate street driving. How well it works on the track would be determined by the heat transfer capacity of the evaporator and condenser, and to a lesser extent the capacity of the compressor to move freon.<BR>     In either situation, a well designed system would maintain IAT at 50 degrees all the time, promoting very consistent performance. Wouldn't you like to still be running cold weather times when every one else is running hot weather times?<BR>     Even if the system doesn't have enough capacity to keep IAT at 50 degrees, it's still cooling the inlet air and doing at least as good a job as a more conventional intercooler.<BR>     You intercooler is a mass of aluminum that is at or above ambient temperature. Don't you think a larger mass of water (much higher specific heat than aluminum) at lower than ambient temp would work better for short bursts on the strip?||||reg||4
Z||000022||zeusrekning||09-12-2000||07:05 PM||||before i post remember i know little about turbo's. My boss is puttuing an after cooler on our air compressor system. The air gets hot don't remember the temp he said but when it enters our newly air conditioned shop it cools rapidly making the water condense and our airlines turn into water lines.<BR>The after cooler is a large radiator with an electric motor on the back. not exactly sure how it works but guessing like a car radiator.||||reg||1
Z||000023||hugh||09-12-2000||07:34 PM||||Will,It sounds like it could work,but I think the cost would be prohibitive and the results minimal.If you can make it work effectively and efficiently I will be the first in line to say you did a good job.You could use a GMC water to air cooler as a starting place.Good luck.Hugh ||||reg||1
Z||000024||lou_dias||09-12-2000||11:58 PM||||What turbo did you put in?  custom or IRM/Design One...<P>You won't be impressed looking at my 3.5...<BR>The only thing that looks different in the engine compartment is the Sprint headers and the Accel coil mounted on the trunk wall.<P>I am usually in Providence saturday nights at the COMPLEX.  Unless you come earlier.<BR>Give me your email address and I'll send you my number.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Carrolles||09-13-2000||09:07 AM||||Did you erev notice how our engines seem to be more peppy on cool damp mornings?  This is the same effect you'll experience with water injection.<P>During WWII the piston engines in military airplanes used water injection for the last resort "get my as* out of here !" performance.  They called it full military power.  It was achieved with water injection.  This lowered combustion chamber head temperatures which allowed for a leaner air/fuel mixture and more power.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Phantom Rage||09-13-2000||09:54 PM||||mrfixit58, Please check your e-mail <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Will, interesting idea.  Keep us updated, I would love to see a working prototype.  <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000027||WKDFIRO||09-14-2000||04:18 AM||||I used to work with Keith Huff at Fiero Motorsports. There was an 88 GT 3.4 0Turbo Air to Water Intercooler with water injection. The intercooler was fitted behind the passenger side intake scoop using a motorcycle radiator with a circulating pump running glycol (engine coolant)in a closed system. Using an Andial gauge to measure the temp of gasses before and after the intercooler we had deltas of around 75 degrees at full boost. <BR>     Let me tell you, a turbo automatic has to be the spookiest kind of fast there is! No turbo lag to speak of and no drop in boost since the automatic takes care of things handily! Nothing but boost all the way! I wrote up the details on this car for Fiero Owners Club of America magazine. I know that there is a pic of it somewhere on the net. The owner is in our club so I have seen it enough times to forget to grab a picture of it for myself. <BR>     Just a few things of note, we had to lower the compression a bit and ran 12 psi with no problems. At 12 psi the wheels are still trying to grab traction from a standstill. Anymore (the concensus) boost was not necessary. ||||reg||1
Z||000028||Standard||09-14-2000||04:44 AM||||Pic of a air-water intercooled setup.. no I don't remember where this came from, or any details..<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/28%20turbo1.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker<P>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 09-14-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 09-14-2000).]||||reg||1
